 "summary": "The new nonsingular-family statements sit at the natural level: the `MemLp`/`Integrable` lemmas are unconditional (handling the off-range zero measure by cases), and the moment identities carry exactly `S.PosDef` and `(p : \u211d) - 1 < n`, both necessary since `nonsingularWishartMeasure` is `0` off that range (`Nonsingular.lean:286-288`) while `n * S i j`, `2 * n * trace (\u0398 * S * \u0398 * S)` and `n \u2022 S` are not. All specializations go through the existing hypothesis-level lemmas with `hS.posSemidef`, so nothing is re-proved for the special case. (The PR description's claim that the hypotheses are kept on the `MemLp`/`Integrable` statements is stale \u2014 the code correctly omits them.)",
